<h2>The case</h2>
<p>The appeal, under Section 21(4) of the NIA Act read with Section 43D(5) UAPA, challenged a Patiala House order of 7 August 2023 rejecting bail in NIA Case No. 2/2022 (RC No. 29/2021/NIA/DLI). The NIA's case paints the appellant as a radicalised recruiter and associate of proscribed outfits who allegedly stored and supplied grenades, handed over AK-47 ammunition, sheltered operatives and channelled funds. The appellant was arrested on 16 December 2021; charges under Sections 120B and 121A IPC and Section 18 UAPA were framed on 30 July 2024 — and, notably, he was discharged of Sections 20, 38 and 39 UAPA and Section 25(1A) Arms Act.</p>

<h2>The arguments in brief</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Appellant</h3>
    <p>Nearly five years in custody with 21 of 356 witnesses examined engages K.A. Najeeb and its line of authority. The disclosure statement led to no discovery attributable to him; the recovery from witness X-12 came 24 days later with no chain of custody; the accomplice evidence of X-1 requires corroboration; and several co-accused have bail — from the trial court, the High Court and the Supreme Court.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>NIA</h3>
    <p>The Section 43D(5) threshold is met on the chargesheet read as a whole (Watali; Gurwinder Singh). Post-order evidence is a matter of trial appreciation. The Supreme Court's May 2024 direction for an expeditious trial answers the delay plea, and the appellant has UAPA antecedents at Sopore.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>What the Bench actually held</h2>
<p>Three points of law emerge. First, following <em>Shoma Kanti Sen</em>, the appellate court may consider material arising after the impugned order — the Bench looked at protected witness X-1's deposition that the appellant handed him a bag of grenades, while holding that its weight should first be assessed at trial. Second, on delay, the court accepted the Article 21 premise but declined to apply it in the abstract: whether the remaining material witnesses — X-12 and the key formal witnesses — can be examined quickly, and why the trial has moved slowly, are questions for the court conducting it. Third, on parity, bail granted to co-accused on delay grounds does not travel automatically; the individual role matters.</p>

<h2>The operative architecture</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1.</strong> Appeal disposed of — no merits finding on the prosecution evidence, expressly.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2.</strong> Liberty to move a fresh bail application before the trial court.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3.</strong> The trial court must decide uninfluenced by the earlier rejection or by observations in this judgment, considering subsequent trial developments.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4.</strong> The application must be decided expeditiously.</div>
</div>
<p>For UAPA practice, that architecture is the story. A remand of this kind resets the record: the fresh application will be judged on the trial as it stands in late 2026 — the pace of witness examination since the 2024 charge-framing, the content of the depositions so far, and the discharge of the appellant from several of the gravest counts.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In long-running UAPA matters, defence teams should treat each tranche of recorded evidence as potential changed-circumstance material, and frame renewed bail applications around what the depositions have and have not established — not around the chargesheet narrative alone. Where health grounds exist, they must be raised before the trial court first; the Bench here declined to consider ailments never placed below.</p>
</div>
